WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:07.360
"Where have you gone, sweet child? It's cold outside. It's awfully cold.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.680
Where have you run off to?

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:13.160
I'm here sweet child, right here.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.320
You're cold and you have no place to go.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.720
You're mine, sweet child. All mine.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.940
So run home, sweet child, run home.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.220
You know you have no place to go."

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:34.120
This piece of missing dialogue almost certainly belongs to the crawling tree, who was unique amongst all her sisters.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.960
For, as you approach, in-game, you can hear her weep.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.500
*soft crying*

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:48.680
And it's this same sound present in the missing dialogue you just heard.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:54.720
So we're left wondering, who then is her sweet child with no place to go?

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:01.040
Guarded by the tree is Snap Freeze, one of the two spells left behind by the young sorcerer Sulyvahn.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:10.760
It reads, "Sulyvahn was born and raised inside the painting, yet had little use for his frigid homeland, since he had not yet experienced loss."

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:18.860
This references his birth, his upbringing, and all that next to a tree that talks about a sweet child who has disappeared.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.900
Of course, it doesn't all add up.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:27.580
It doesn't make sense that the tree is outside yet talks about it being cold outside.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:34.580
It doesn't make sense that items say, "Sulyvahn has no place to go", yet the tree tells him to run home.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:42.700
I'll be honest - I just wanted a segway to talk about Sulyvahn because we didn't actually get a chance to talk about him in the previous videos

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:50.600
We learn a little bit more about him in the painting, and I think it might help us understand the man he became.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.100
For the painted world is essentially one big mirror to the linking of the fire

00:01:55.100 --> 00:02:01.780
so it's no surprise that Sulyvahn may have been quick to grasp the nature of the outside world.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:10.180
Within the painting, the residents pride themselves on their ability to paint a new world once their existing world has fallen to rot.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.320
And in a way, Sulyvahn sets out to do that same thing on the outside.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.380
The outside, too... it's rotting to these endless ages of fire.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:29.640
And Sulyvahn does fight against that in a way, because he joins forces with Aldrich, this being who has dreamt of an end to the age of fire,

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.080
and of the beginning of the age of the Deep Sea. We'll get to that in a second.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:39.780
Going back to the painting... while he was there, Sulyvahn was classed as a young sorcerer

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:44.000
with sorceries that were clearly inspired by his homeland.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:55.580
However, the items read, "Ironically, he did not belong in this place of belonging, as he had not yet experienced loss, unlike the others in the painting."

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:59.940
So he left for a similar environment, but a different world.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.800
Irithyll, located in the Boreal Valley, was where he discovered the Profaned Flame deep below,

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.040
where he found his ambition - where he became the Pontiff.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:15.980
Eventually usurping the old gods from Anor Lando, and eventually taking the frozen city for his own.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:22.500
He then goes on to provide refuge for Aldrich, a Lord of Cinder who abandoned his duty to link the fire,

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:27.820
and even offers him Gwendolyn, a god of the old royalty, to devour.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:32.680
And I think knowing his origins flesh out the character a little bit more.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:39.460
They help us understand the man who settled in Irithyll – why he might have rejected the rotten linking of the fire,

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:46.060
and eventually how he probably wanted to be a part of a new world, just like the Corvians of the painting.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:47.680
Moving on.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:53.480
This is a texture of the murals in Ariandel, courtesy of @MennoBarten on Twitter.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:58.520
Now Jake, The Ashen Hollow, managed to find some hidden text in this texture.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.560
I’m gonna give you a few seconds, and see if you can find it, too.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:04.700
It’s here.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:13.680
Forgive my pronunciation, but *slaughtered japanese* roughly translates to, “Stabber and Crusher.”

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:20.740
Stabber and Crusher. Thanks to our resident translator Loremaster Nojah once again for that.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:28.940
So the discovery of this collaborative treasure hunt is that the two figures are clearly called Stabber and Crusher,

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:35.580
perhaps internally by From Software, which is probably not intended for our knowledge to be honest.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:44.080
You’d think it’s just their name, but it reveals that the act of stabbing and crushing might be an important part of the mural,

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.940
as well as making it clear that what they’re stabbing and what they’re crushing is alive.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:59.220
Which is very significant because it essentially confirms to me that the two white things are most likely snakes, as you guys suggested in an earlier video.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:03.780
Because why would you stab or crush something that’s not living, right?

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:08.980
Plus, there are two entwined serpents engraved upon the doors to the chapel.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:12.120
So three questions arise for me:

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:18.800
What are the snakes? Who are the figures, and why are they stabbing and crushing the snakes?

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.420
The snakes are most likely primordial serpents.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:32.360
It fits best, because there are two here, and because these two snakes would line up perfectly with the two prominent primordial serpents in the game:

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:38.520
Kaathe and Frampt, who both represented a side of linking the fire in Dark Souls 1.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:47.280
Frampt pushed you to sacrifice yourself for the flame, and Kaathe wanted you to watch it die. He wanted you to usher in the next age of man.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:54.800
And these two snakes, and light and dark, they’re always entwined somehow, just like the two on the door.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:06:07.080
And it does make some sense for these two serpents to be represented in Ariandel, for this is the DLC where Miyazaki has attempted to display the linking of the fire from another perspective.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:11.700
So now, who are the figures, both of whom are fairly feminine?

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:19.980
I’d like to say that they are the two other sisters of the Sable Church, Frieda being the third, but as we’ve already seen two other sisters,

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:27.220
and we know that their masks don’t look anything like this, that assumption definitely comes with some contradictions.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:33.020
So, I personally think that, as of yet, they are unmentioned characters in the game.

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:42.540
Their defining characteristics are one has a straight thin sword, another has an embered arm reaching down to the snakes coming from the dark,

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:49.140
who are likely the primordial serpents. Hollows enwreathed in flame reach out towards them…

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:55.920
for help, perhaps? In this pose that is so familiar to other praying hollows in the game.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:04.380
And if these are primordial serpents, that makes these two characters huge by comparison, and probably powerful, too.

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:08.500
And the third question: why are they stabbing and crushing the snakes?

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:16.020
Well, if we assume that the snakes are Kaathe and Frampt, then killing them might represent animosity for the linking of the fire,

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:24.200
which is something that both Kaathe and Frampt represent in a way, as each serpent pushes you to either link the fire or usurp it.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:32.520
And Friede is unkindled – she is someone who failed to link or usurp the fire, and she fled to the painting after that failure.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:43.040
In my opinion, she’d be quite okay with the deaths of Frampt or Kaathe, and the depiction of them within this place makes a bit of sense with that context.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:48.200
Of course, just because I’ve mentioned the most likely explanations, doesn’t mean it’s right.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:53.840
Next, the woman in the tens of paintings scattered around Friede’s chamber.

00:07:54.320 --> 00:08:05.080
I think it’s clear that it's Friede herself depicted, as the location and the pose is the same as where and how she sits in real life – hands clasped, with a round table by her side.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:11.280
It’s like she’s posing for these pictures, which were presumably done by the painter upstairs.

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:13.620
But why have so many done?

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:21.920
Well, there is something special about the paintings made by the painter, since she is responsible for creating the next world.

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:32.140
Maybe Friede wanted to affirm her position here in this world with a bit of the painter’s power. But we know she sent this painter away eventually.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:35.020
Maybe she wasn’t happy with her depiction in these works.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:40.840
Maybe it has something to do with the fire that encroaches around the edges of each painting.

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:48.920
The fire was unavoidably linked to Friede, no matter how much she despised it, she couldn’t separate herself from it in the end.

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:56.740
In one picture in particular, Friede holds a tablet in her bandaged hands with a knight depicted upon it.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:05.260
This knight has a helm that’s unlike anything we’ve seen in-game, but it also has what looks like the woolen pauldrons of the Faraam set,

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:08.660
and what looks like the plume of the Nameless King behind it.

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:10.060
I’m reaching here.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:13.280
Those two things are related to each other, but I’m still reaching.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:23.180
We were never meant to look this closely at this mystery knight, I think, so for now, I guess I will just keep an eye out for anything looking like this in the future.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:31.480
Although, it does bring to mind Yuria’s dialog saying that we should remember those that stayed by Friede’s side until the very end.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:39.680
I think it would make more sense for Vilhelm to be depicted in this picture rather than this mystery knight, but that’s just my take.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:47.960
Finally, the textures reveal a burn on Friede’s face, and along with the bandages in the paintings that are around her hands;

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:52.440
maybe these are burn marks from linking the fire and failing, perhaps?

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:55.440
More questions remain that might never get answered.

00:09:56.120 --> 00:10:01.240
Questions like, “What is the Abyss Dragon mentioned in the downfall of the Millwood Knights?”

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:05.600
“What is the snake statue hidden at the back of Ariandel’s chamber?” and

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:12.340
“What is the Dark Soul of Man?” which is the biggest cliff-hanger a piece of DLC has ever left us on.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:22.180
I think as From prepares their final piece of DLC for the Dark Souls series, and as it increasingly looks like we’re going to visit a city of hollows,

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:29.720
I think that we are going to have to come to term with the fact that we may never have these questions answered for sure.

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:33.180
But regardless, it’s been damn fun speculating.

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:39.460
And, just before I go, I wanna say that we actually have more of a community starting over on Patreon right now,

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:45.220
so everyone who gives a dollar now has access to the Discord channel, which has been so much fun!

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:49.920
There’s a place you can show off your art, like Emily Hickinson’s work you can see here.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:54.620
There’s general discussion, there’s lore discussion, you can link music, pictures...

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:59.780
If you’re looking for a Dark Souls community to be a part of, I’m having a blast with this.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:07.560
And I’m adding a few more perks, too, so five dollar patrons can now be polled to help me decide what direction to take the channel,

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:14.100
and I’ve even started to upload the notes that I make my videos based off, which are for Beacon patrons.

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:19.940
These notes have color-coded research on dialogue, item descriptions, environments, and

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:27.880
basically if you want access to the rough unedited core of my videos, then this tier might be interesting to you.

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:32.880
But thank you for supporting me, whether you’re a patron, or you’re not a patron, it’s all good!

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:35.940
Thank you for watching this, and I’ll see you next time!

